# Scenario
Learn the tools used to aid an analyst to investigate suspicious emails. As a level 1 SOC analyst, investigate multiple suspicious emails escalated by coworkers, extract key indicators of compromise, and identify signs of malicious activity to help the team develop detection rules.

# Tools Used
- TryHackMe Attack Box
- Thunderbird (email client / message source viewer)
- ANYRUN (online malware sandbox)
- Phish3Case1.eml (task file)

# What I Did

**Case 1 — Your Account is On Hold (Netflix phishing)**

Step 1 — Identify the impersonated brand
- Opened Phish3Case1.eml in Thunderbird on the Attack Box.
- Examined the From field:
```
Netflix<JGQ47wazXe1xYVBrkeDg-JOg7ODDQwWdR@JOg7ODDQwWdR-yVkCaBkTNp.gogolecloud.com>
```
- Subject line read: Y o u r N e t f l i x A c c o u n t i s o n H o l d
- Spacing between characters in the subject is a deliberate obfuscation technique to bypass keyword filters.
- Confirmed impersonated brand: Netflix

Step 2 — Identify the intended recipient
- Checked the To field and found the recipient: redacted@yahoo.com

Step 3 — Extract the originating IP from headers
- Used View > Message Source in Thunderbird to inspect raw headers.
- Found the originating IP in the Received field:
```
Received: from 10.197.37.234
```

Step 4 — Identify the domain of interest from Return-Path
- Located the Return-Path field in the message source:
```
Return-Path: <postmaster@etekno.xyz>
```
- Domain of interest: etekno.xyz (not associated with Netflix)

Step 5 — Investigate the Update Account Now button
- Inspected the link behind the Update Account Now call to action in the email body.
- Found a shortened URL used to obscure the final destination: https://t.co/yuxfZm8KPg?amp=1

