# Key Learning
Phishing emails impersonating reputable brands frequently use lookalike sending domains and obfuscated subject lines to bypass automated filters. Character spacing within subject lines such as "Y o u r N e t f l i x" is a deliberate keyword evasion technique that analysts must recognise visually rather than relying on automated detection alone. Sandbox tools like ANYRUN are essential for safely detonating suspicious attachments and observing real-time process behaviour, network connections, and threat classifications without risking live infrastructure. CVE-2017-11882, despite being patched in 2017, remains actively exploited in phishing campaigns through malicious Excel files targeting unpatched systems running the legacy Equation Editor.

# What I Would Flag in a Real SOC
- A sender domain that does not match the impersonated brand (gogolecloud.com vs netflix.com) should be blocked at the email gateway and the sending IP added to the deny list
- The Return-Path domain etekno.xyz differing from the display From address is a strong email spoofing indicator and should trigger an SPF and DMARC verification check
- Shortened URLs embedded in email call-to-action buttons bypass URL reputation checks and should be expanded and sandboxed before delivery, URL shorteners in email bodies are a high-confidence phishing indicator
- AcroRd32.exe making outbound connections to flagged IPs on port 443 indicates the PDF is attempting to beacon or download a secondary payload, PDF reader processes should not be making unsolicited outbound connections
- EQNEDT32.EXE spawning any child process or making network connections is a definitive indicator of CVE-2017-11882 exploitation, this process should be blocked from executing entirely on any patched system
- The presence of COVID19.exe as a filename in a download URL is a social engineering lure and all executable downloads from unknown domains should be blocked at the proxy or firewall egress layer
